# A.C.E. — Cursor project rules

You are building A.C.E., an international competitive esports platform focused first on Counter-Strike 2.

Read `docs/PRODUCT_SPEC.md`, `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/ROADMAP.md`, `docs/SECURITY.md`, and `docs/DECISIONS.md` before large changes. Follow the current roadmap stage. Do not build the entire platform in one task.

## Product identity

- Name: A.C.E.
- Brand: Autonomous Anti-Cheat, Competitive Calibration, Elite Players.
- Public UI language: English.
- Colors: charcoal #121212, graphite gray, white, gold #D4AF37 / #FFD700.
- Audience: international competitive players.

## Honest claims (never violate)

A.C.E. does not have a working AI anti-cheat, live game servers, real payments, or real prizes. Do not write copy or features that claim otherwise. Mark mock data, future features, and unfinished integrations clearly.

Never store Steam passwords or ask users to enter Steam passwords on A.C.E. Steam sign-in must use Steam’s official OpenID / API flow only.

Do not make paid purchases, deploy paid services, or incur costs without explicit authorization from the project owner.

## Technology

- Next.js App Router + TypeScript in `ace-platform`. Source lives under `src/`.
- Tailwind CSS v4 for styling. Do not add a second UI kit without recording it in `docs/DECISIONS.md`.
- PostgreSQL + a stable Prisma release when database work begins — not before the roadmap stage.
- Do not introduce prerelease dependencies without explaining why in `DECISIONS.md`.
- Do not introduce Electron until the web application is stable (see roadmap Stage 12).
- Do not add unnecessary libraries or duplicate functionality.
- This Next.js version may differ from older tutorials. Read `AGENTS.md` and `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before using unfamiliar APIs.
- Leave `AGENTS.md` in place (Next.js regenerates it).

## Development rules

1. Work in small, clearly defined stages from `docs/ROADMAP.md`.
2. Never attempt to build the entire platform in one task.
3. Before changing files, inspect the existing project. Do not overwrite working code unnecessarily.
4. Do not invent successful tests. Run available checks (`npm run lint`, `npm run build`, and `npm test` only if it exists) and report actual results.
5. When a test or check fails, explain the cause and fix it before claiming success.
6. Keep README commands exact for install, run, lint, build, and start.
7. Keep secrets out of source. Add `.env.example` with placeholders only when env vars are needed. Then allow `.env.example` in `.gitignore` (`!.env.example`).
8. Enforce security-sensitive checks on the server, not only in the browser.
9. Prefer git checkpoints before major changes when git is available. Do not commit unless the user asks.
10. Create folders (`src/components`, `src/lib`, `src/server`) only when a stage needs them.

## Implementation style

- Explain important decisions in plain language. The owner is not an experienced programmer.
- Every implementation step must be testable (browser path, lint, build, or a written checklist).
- Path alias: `@/*` → `./src/*`.
